1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Creating one file is four separate disk writes, and the power can fail between any two of them.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Ten crash points: one before any write, then one after each of the nine in-place writes.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Six of the ten leave a disk that no longer makes sense. Three break metadata, three break the bytes.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Only one crash point out of ten leaves all three operations complete and correct.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
The write-ahead rule: log the new image of every block, and never overwrite a home block first.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
The commit record is one block, and it is the atomic point of the whole transaction.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Then the images are copied home, and only then is the log space freed.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
So a crash gives recovery exactly two choices: the transaction happened, or it did not.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
The same three transactions, four commit disciplines, and a crash at every single write point.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Without a journal, six of the ten crash points are broken: three metadata, three bytes.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Metadata-only journaling fixes every metadata invariant — at all twenty-five crash points, both orders.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
But writeback still exposes stale bytes at seven crash points, and ordered exposes none.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Recovery is three passes: find the end of the log, find the last commit, redo the images.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
A transaction that never committed is discarded, and the image equals the last committed state.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Replay is idempotent: doing it twice leaves exactly the image doing it once leaves.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
And recovery reads the log, not the disk: sixty-four blocks instead of two hundred sixty-two thousand.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
For m metadata blocks a journaled transaction costs 2m + 3 block writes instead of m.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
The log writes each block twice, and it writes it sequentially into one contiguous file.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
A sixty-four block journal holds sixteen transactions of two metadata blocks each.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
One transaction must fit whole, so a sixty-four block journal takes at most sixty-two metadata blocks.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
All three ext4 data modes journal the metadata. They differ only in where the data block sits.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Full data journaling protects the bytes, and costs two writes per data block as well.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Ordered mode adds one rule: the data reaches its home block before the commit record does.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Writeback commits earlier — write point four instead of six for the first transaction.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
One transaction, three in-place writes, six possible orders — and the order decides everything.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Three of the six are unsafe, and they are exactly the ones that write the inode before the data.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
On the whole workload, writeback exposes stale bytes at seven of its twenty-five crash points.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Writeback is durable at five crash points and intact at only two. Ordered makes the two coincide.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
A journal is not a backup. It undoes half-done work, never wrong work.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
It does not check your data. It never compares a block with what it should contain.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
It does not survive a broken log. A media error leaves nothing to replay.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
And it is not free: seven writes instead of two at two metadata blocks, plus a periodic flush.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Shape one: how many block writes does this operation cost? Count 2m + 3, or m without a journal.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Shape two: how many blocks does recovery read? The log, not the disk.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Shape three: which ordering rule is safe? The one that puts the data block before the commit.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Shape four: what does the commit record make atomic? Shape five: what does this crash point leave?

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Trap one: a journaling file system is not a log-structured file system.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Trap two: a committed transaction does not mean your data is safe.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Trap three: replaying the journal twice does not corrupt anything.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Trap four: journaling does not delete the consistency check. It moves it off the normal path.

